<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.1.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- controller watchdog (#2303): restart a controller that is ALIVE BUT NOT CYCLING, once,
         and STOP restarting when restarting cannot help.

         ⛔ WHY (measured 2026-10-21 on this box). The controller was up and wedged for a full hour.
         `launchctl` said the job was loaded and `harness.sh verify` said GREEN while the supervisor
         logged `controller pid … is alive but the heartbeat is 3652s old` every 30s — restarting it
         into the SAME crash (a macOS bash 3.2 parse failure, agent-infra#2248) and dispatching
         nothing. Nothing in the fleet looked at the one datum that differs between a healthy box and
         that box: whether $O/heartbeat ADVANCED. A process-existence predicate cannot see it, and a
         predicate that only logs it (the supervisor's) cannot stop it.

         THE CONTRACT THE SCRIPT IMPLEMENTS (scripts/orchestrator-harness/watchdog-loop.sh, pinned by
         watchdog-loop.test.sh):
           fresh heartbeat + pid   → HEALTHY, nothing touched
           pid alive + stale hb    → WEDGED  → exactly ONE restart, re-checked after SETTLE
           no pid + stale hb       → DOWN    → one restart
           no usable heartbeat     → DOWN    (never healthy)
           N consecutive wedges    → STOP restarting and record ONE crash line quoting
                                     $O/logs/controller-stdout.log, so the repeating crash is visible
                                     instead of restarted forever.

         ⛔ THE TARGET IS THE CHECKOUT'S SCRIPT, NOT A FARMED COPY, so the `<string>` resolves on a
         FRESH box (install-launchd.sh hard-fails a job whose target does not resolve, and that check
         must not be defeated). {{AGENT_INFRA_PATH}} is this repo's MAIN checkout — worktree-safe, so
         a passing worktree's teardown cannot leave this job pointing at nothing.

         ⛔ THE BOUNDS ARE IN THE PLIST, NOT INHERITED. A launchd job gets neither the operator's
         shell env nor its rc files, so an unset variable silently keeps the script's default — and
         for THIS job the defaults are the contract. They are written out for that reason:
           HB_STALE=780  the supervisor's own limit (CYC*2+300 at CYC=240). A watchdog that waited
                         LONGER than the supervisor's report would let the box sit wedged for the
                         report's duration plus its own window; a SHORTER one would restart a healthy
                         controller mid-cycle, which is the P0 the supervisor refuses to do.
           SETTLE=60     the re-check window after a restart. The loop writes $O/heartbeat as the
                         FIRST act of a cycle, so a restored controller proves itself in seconds and
                         a crash-on-start is still unambiguous at 60s.
           MAX_CONSEC=3  three consecutive wedges, i.e. three restarts that each failed to restore
                         cycling, then the restart path is CLOSED and the crash is recorded. Without
                         this the job is the measured hour, on a timer.

         ⛔ EXIT 0 ALWAYS. launchd's last-exit-status is read by nobody, so a non-zero exit from a
         watchdog is invisible exactly when it matters. The decision is the OUTPUT: one line per pass
         in {{HOME}}/.pi/agent/state/controller-watchdog.log (bounded, rotated with a line saying
         so), plus the crash record at .../controller-watchdog.crash. the script's own `status` verb
         reprints both, which is the surface a human and `status` read.
         The launchd capture below is only for a death BEFORE the script can log anything (a bash
         syntax error in the script itself, a missing interpreter) — the same class that wedged the
         controller, so the watchdog must not reproduce it silently.

         ⛔ ON A LINUX CELL there is no launchd and this template is not installed; the script's own
         restart branch calls the cell supervisor's `start` verb there, which is the same verb the
         cell doctor role uses to bring a controller back — no second launch path is invented. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.eldato.controller-watchdog</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>{{AGENT_INFRA_PATH}}/scripts/orchestrator-harness/watchdog-loop.sh</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>{{HOME}}</string>
        <key>O</key>
        <string>{{HOME}}/orchestrator</string>
        <key>HB_STALE</key>
        <string>780</string>
        <key>SETTLE</key>
        <string>60</string>
        <key>MAX_CONSEC</key>
        <string>3</string>
    </dict>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <!-- 300s = 5 min. The supervisor's own tick is 30s and its limit is 780s, so a 5-minute pass
         detects a wedge within ~13-18 minutes of it starting — while the heartbeat datum only moves
         once per controller cycle (240s), so a tighter timer would read the same instant again.
         This is the cadence the live box was hand-armed at. -->
    <integer>300</integer>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/state/controller-watchdog.launchd.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/state/controller-watchdog.launchd.err</string>
</dict>
</plist>
